Thought: To identify the SMILES string of the organic compound, I should first gather some initial data about the compound. The mass spectrometry spectra can provide the molecular weight and potential fragments, which is a good starting point for identifying the compound. This will help in narrowing down the possible structures.
Action: mass_spectrometry_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The mass spectrometry data indicates a molecular ion peak at m/z 211.076, which is likely the molecular weight of the compound. To proceed, I should determine the molecular formula that corresponds to this molecular weight. This will help in identifying possible isomers and narrowing down the SMILES string.
Action: retrieve_isotope_distribution
Action Input: {}

Thought: The isotopic distribution data provides information on the isotopes of common elements, which can help in deducing the molecular formula. Given the molecular ion peak at m/z 211.076, I should now calculate the possible molecular formula that matches this mass. This will allow me to explore potential isomers and their SMILES strings.
Action: retrieve_dbe_formula
Action Input: {}

Thought: With the DBE formula, I can calculate the degree of unsaturation for potential molecular formulas that match the molecular weight of 211.076. This will help in identifying the structure of the compound. To proceed, I should first obtain the molecular formula that corresponds to this mass.
Action: obtain_isomers_from_molecular_formula
Action Input: {"molecular_formula": "C13H13NO2"}

Thought: I have obtained a list of possible isomers for the molecular formula C13H13NO2. To identify the correct SMILES string, I should compare the NMR spectra of the sample with the predicted spectra for these isomers. I will start by obtaining the proton NMR spectra of the sample to compare with the isomers.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Since the proton NMR prediction failed, I should try obtaining the carbon NMR spectra of the sample. This can also provide valuable information to differentiate between the isomers.
Action: carbon_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Both proton and carbon NMR predictions have failed. As an alternative approach, I can use the IR spectra to identify functional groups present in the compound, which might help in distinguishing between the isomers.
Action: ir_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The IR spectra show peaks at 1707 cm⁻¹, which is indicative of a carbonyl group (C=O), and peaks in the range of 1595-1579 cm⁻¹, which suggest the presence of aromatic rings. This information can help narrow down the isomers. I will now validate the SMILES strings of the isomers to ensure they are correct and then compare them with the IR data to find the best match.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC(C(=O)N)OC1=CC=CC2=CC=CC=C21"}

Thought: The SMILES string "CC(C(=O)N)OC1=CC=CC2=CC=CC=C21" is valid. This structure contains a carbonyl group and aromatic rings, which matches the IR spectra data. I will now validate the other isomers to ensure I have considered all possibilities.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C1=CC=NC=C1.C1=COC=C1.C1=COC=C1"}

Thought: The SMILES string "C1=CC=NC=C1.C1=COC=C1.C1=COC=C1" is also valid, but it represents a mixture of separate aromatic rings and does not contain a carbonyl group, which does not match the IR data. I will validate the next isomer.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC1=C2C(=CC=C1)C(=C(C(=N2)C)C)C(=O)O"}

Thought: The SMILES string "CC1=C2C(=CC=C1)C(=C(C(=N2)C)C)C(=O)O" is valid and contains both a carbonyl group and aromatic rings, which matches the IR data. I will validate the next isomer to ensure all possibilities are considered.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C1[C@@H]2[C@H]1C(=O)N3[C@H]2CO[C@H]3C4=CC=CC=C4"}

Thought: The SMILES string "C1[C@@H]2[C@H]1C(=O)N3[C@H]2CO[C@H]3C4=CC=CC=C4" is valid and also contains a carbonyl group and aromatic rings, matching the IR data. I will validate the final isomer to complete the analysis.
Action: validate_smiles
